Grape Lua API
=============

Scripts are Lua 5.4 and live in the Grape/scripts folder. Open Settings → Open Grape Folder, drop a .lua file in scripts, then load it from the Scripts tab.

Lifecycle
---------

```lua
function on_load() end
function on_update(dt) end
function on_input(player, button, pressed) end
function on_notification(title, body, id) end
function on_draw() end
function on_overlay() end
function on_unload() end
```

All callbacks are optional. on_update runs once per rendered game frame. on_input runs on every click/release before the game handles it; player is 1 or 2 and button is jump (1), left (2), or right (3). on_notification runs once for every notification raised anywhere — by this script, by another one, by Grape itself, or pushed from the server — which is what makes it useful for reacting to an announcement rather than only for confirming your own. on_draw renders inside the Scripts tab. on_overlay renders over the level and shaders but under the Grape menu. An error or instruction-limit violation stops that script and shows the error in the menu.

Game API
--------

```lua
grape.log(message)
grape.notify(title[, body][, options]) -> id
grape.frame() -> integer
grape.in_level() -> boolean
grape.tps([value]) -> number
grape.speed([value]) -> number
grape.input(player, button, pressed)
grape.player_get(player, field) -> value
grape.player_set(player, field, value)
grape.trajectory(player, action, delay, frames) -> prediction
```

player is 1 or 2. button is 1 (jump), 2 (left), or 3 (right). Supported readable player fields: x, y, rotation, y_velocity, x_velocity, gravity, scale, opacity, visible, dead, holding, upside_down, going_left, cube, ship, ufo, ball, wave, robot, spider, swing and platformer. Writable fields: x, y, rotation, y_velocity, x_velocity, gravity, scale, opacity and visible.

Notifications
-------------

```lua
grape.notify(title[, body][, options]) -> id
grape.notify.show(title[, body][, options]) -> id
grape.notify.update(id[, title][, body]) -> boolean
grape.notify.color(id, r, g, b[, a]) -> boolean
grape.notify.dismiss(id) -> boolean
grape.notify.clear()
```

Raises the toast that slides in from the bottom-right of the screen, in the menu's own surface, border and corner radius, with a coloured rule down its left edge. options is { color = {r,g,b[,a]}, seconds = n }; colours are 0-1 and seconds is 2-600. Leave color out and the rule takes the player's own menu accent, which is usually what you want — a notification is rarely important enough to override someone's theme. An alpha of zero means the same thing, so you can hand a toast back to the accent after setting a colour.

The id is what makes the rest of the table useful: update and color edit a toast that is already on screen instead of raising a second one, which is how a progress or status toast is built. Four toasts are shown at once and older ones slide out early rather than queueing. grape.notify stays callable as a plain function, so scripts written against the old single-argument form keep working.

```lua
local toast

function on_draw()
  if grape.ui.button("Start") then
    toast = grape.notify("Converting", "Starting…", { seconds = 60 })
  end
end

function on_update(dt)
  if toast and working then
    grape.notify.update(toast, nil, math.floor(percent) .. "% done")
    grape.notify.color(toast, 1 - percent / 100, percent / 100, 0.2, 1)
  end
end

function on_notification(title, body, id)
  grape.log("saw #" .. id .. ": " .. title)
end
```

Audio
-----

```lua
grape.audio.play(file[, options]) -> handle | nil
grape.audio.play_sfx(name[, options]) -> handle | nil
grape.audio.stop(handle) -> boolean
grape.audio.stop_all()
grape.audio.volume(handle[, value]) -> number
grape.audio.pitch(handle[, value]) -> number
grape.audio.position(handle[, seconds]) -> number
grape.audio.length(handle) -> number
grape.audio.paused(handle[, value]) -> boolean
grape.audio.playing(handle) -> boolean
```

play reads .mp3, .ogg, .wav and .flac from the scripts/sounds folder; play_sfx plays one of Geometry Dash's own effects by file name, resolved through the game's own search paths so a texture pack that replaces a sound is honoured. Both take plain file names — no folders, no .., no drive letters — and return a handle, or nil when the file is not there.

options is { volume = 0..1, pitch = 0.01..10, start = seconds, loop = false }. Everything is applied before the first sample is heard, so a sound meant to be quiet does not crack at full volume first. Files over a megabyte are streamed rather than decoded into memory; that choice is made from the size rather than asked of the script.

A script may have 32 sounds going at once. Finished sounds are released for you, a script can only touch handles it started, and unloading or reloading a script stops everything it was playing.

```lua
local music

function on_draw()
  if grape.ui.button("Play") then
    music = grape.audio.play("track.mp3", { volume = 0.5, loop = true })
  end
  if music and grape.audio.playing(music) then
    grape.ui.text(string.format("%.1fs / %.1fs",
      grape.audio.position(music), grape.audio.length(music)))
  end
end
```

3D models
---------

```lua
grape.model.draw(file, options)
grape.model.info(file) -> table | nil, error
grape.model.unload(file) -> boolean

-- info = { triangles, vertices, unit, min, max, clipped,
--          materials, parts, images, textures,
--          has_colors, has_uvs, base_color }
```

Loads a .glb or .gltf from the scripts/models folder and draws it into the level from on_overlay. Triangle geometry and the colour on it are read: the scene graph and its node transforms, positions, normals, vertex colours, texture coordinates, base-colour factors and base-colour textures, and indices in any width. The rest of the PBR model — metallic-roughness, normal, occlusion and emissive maps — along with skins, morph targets, animation and the compression extensions, is not: the draw is a flat-shaded triangle list with no lighting model to feed those into. Buffers and images may be packed into the container, inline as base64, or beside the model on disk.

options is { x, y, size, rotation = {rx,ry,rz}, color = {r,g,b,a}, textured, distance, wireframe, shade, cull, light = {x,y,z}, width }. x and y are the level coordinates the model is anchored at and size is how many level units its longest axis spans — every model is normalised into a unit cube when it loads, so size means the same thing whatever units it was exported in. rotation is radians. distance is the camera distance in model widths; 0 gives an orthographic projection.

textured is on by default and draws the model in its own colours: base-colour factors, vertex colours and base-colour textures, multiplied together the way glTF defines them, with color acting as a tint over the top — so white leaves a model exactly as it was exported. Turn textured off to get a flat model in color alone, which is what a silhouette or a marker wants. Materials in MASK mode are cut out rather than drawn transparent, so cut-out foliage and decals read correctly instead of as grey rectangles.

Colours come out of the file in the space it stores them in: glTF states base-colour factors and vertex colours as linear, and they are converted to sRGB once when the model loads. That conversion is not a nicety — an ordinary dark material handed over as linear arrives looking black. Textures are left alone, since a PNG is already sRGB. A material whose texture has no coordinates to map it onto falls back to its base colour rather than sampling one texel over the whole model.

info reports what the file actually turned out to contain — materials, parts, images, how many resolved to a texture, whether it has vertex colours and texture coordinates, and the first material's base colour. A model that draws in the wrong colour is nearly always one carrying less than it looks like it should, and that is the line that says which.

Models are projected onto the level's own coordinates and then through world_to_screen, which is what makes one behave like part of the level rather than a sticker on the window: it moves, scales and rotates with the camera, and a model anchored to an object stays on it through a zoom trigger. Triangles are drawn furthest-first, because a draw list has no depth buffer. Up to 8000 triangles are drawn per call; info reports clipped = true when a model is larger than that.

```lua
function on_overlay()
  if not grape.in_level() then return end
  local x = grape.player_get(1, "x")
  local y = grape.player_get(1, "y")
  grape.model.draw("ship.glb", {
    x = x, y = y + 120, size = 70,
    rotation = { 0.5, grape.frame() / 240, 0 },
    color = { 0.62, 0.36, 0.88, 0.92 },
  })
end
```

Trajectory probes
-----------------

```lua
local p = grape.trajectory(1, "click", 3, 90)
-- p = { x, y, frames, min_y, max_y, survived }
```

trajectory simulates the selected player for frames physics ticks and flips jump input after delay ticks. action is click or release, 0 <= delay < frames <= 600. survived is true when the player reaches the full horizon. Call probes from on_update and cache their result for drawing; each call runs real Grape trajectory physics.

Use Grape menu controls
-----------------------

```lua
grape.menu.list() -> { tags... }
grape.menu.register(name, default) -> tag
grape.menu.get(tag) -> boolean | number | string
grape.menu.set(tag, value)
grape.menu.toggle(tag) -> boolean
grape.menu.press(tag)
grape.menu.mode(["stopped" | "recording" | "playing"]) -> string
```

register publishes a boolean of your own into the same registry, under script.<script name>.<name>, and returns the tag. That tag is the point: a control in the registry can be bound to a key, which is the only way to turn something in a script on and off without opening the menu — and opening the menu mid-attempt is exactly what you cannot do. Re-registering the same name after a reload finds the existing control again, keeping its key binding and its value.

This is the same shared control registry used by Grape's menu and keybinds, not a copied Lua settings list. list returns every available tag in the current build. get/set works with every registered boolean, number, and string control; set runs the control's normal change handler. toggle changes a checkbox. press activates one-shot boolean controls such as updater.advance_one or updater.advance_back. mode reads or changes the recorder/playback buttons.

```lua
for _, tag in ipairs(grape.menu.list()) do grape.log(tag) end
grape.menu.set("updater.tps", 360)
grape.menu.toggle("hitboxes.enabled")
grape.menu.press("updater.advance_one")
grape.menu.mode("recording")
```

Script-tab UI
-------------

```lua
grape.ui.text(text)
grape.ui.button(label) -> boolean
grape.ui.checkbox(label, value) -> boolean
grape.ui.slider(label, value, min, max) -> number
grape.ui.input(label, value) -> number
grape.ui.same_line()
grape.ui.separator()
grape.ui.image(file, width, height)
grape.ui.corner_image(file, corner, width, height, padding)
```

Images are relative to the scripts folder. Put shared files in scripts/resources. PNG, JPEG and animated GIF are decoded once and cached. corner is top_left, top_right, bottom_left or bottom_right; corner_image must be called from on_overlay. Keep labels unique by adding ##id when two controls show the same text.

Level drawing
-------------

```lua
grape.level.world_to_screen(x, y) -> screen_x, screen_y, visible, scale
grape.level.screen_to_world(screen_x, screen_y) -> x, y
grape.level.to_screen({x1,y1,...}) -> {sx1,sy1,...}
grape.level.camera() -> { x, y, width, height, zoom, rotation }
grape.level.line(x1, y1, x2, y2, r, g, b[, a, width])
grape.level.polyline({x1,y1,x2,y2,...}, r,g,b[,a,closed,width])
grape.level.polygon({x1,y1,x2,y2,...}, r,g,b[,a,filled,width])
grape.level.rect(x1,y1,x2,y2,r,g,b[,a,filled,width])
grape.level.circle(x,y,radius,r,g,b[,a,filled,width,segments])
grape.level.text(x,y,text,size,r,g,b[,a,centered])
grape.level.image(file,x,y,width,height[,alpha])
```

world_to_screen also returns scale: how many screen pixels one level unit is worth at that point, which is what lets a script drawing in screen space size things to the level instead of being wrong the moment the camera zooms. screen_to_world is its inverse, through the same transform. to_screen converts a whole shape in one call, which matters because converting a hundred points one at a time costs a hundred Lua calls a frame. camera reports the visible region in level units and is derived from the same transform, so it stays right through zoom triggers, camera rotation and static-camera sections. These four are queries rather than drawing, so unlike the rest of this table they can be called from on_update as well as on_overlay.

All coordinates, sizes, and widths are level/world units and follow the complete camera transform. Colors and alpha are 0-1. Call drawing functions from on_overlay. Polygons are convex; combine multiple polygons for concave shapes. Images support PNG, JPEG, and animated GIF files relative to the script. These primitives can compose arbitrary level overlays rather than only circles.

```lua
function on_overlay()
  if not grape.in_level() then return end
  grape.level.line(100, 100, 220, 160, 1, 0.3, 0.2, 1, 2)
  grape.level.polygon({240,100, 290,170, 340,100}, 0.3,0.8,1,0.7,true)
  grape.level.rect(360,100,430,160, 1,1,0,1,false,2)
  grape.level.circle(480,130,30, 0.7,0.2,1,0.8,false,3)
  grape.level.text(540,130,"Lua",18, 1,1,1,1,true)
  grape.level.image("resources/marker.png",600,100,64,64,0.9)
end
```

Reading the loaded macro
------------------------

```lua
grape.macro.length() -> integer
grape.macro.position() -> integer
grape.macro.tps([frame]) -> number
grape.macro.platformer() -> boolean
grape.macro.seed() -> number
grape.macro.name() -> string
grape.macro.event(index) -> table | nil
```

The recording itself, which nothing else in the API exposes. event is one-based and returns { frame, type, holding, player2, swift }, plus tps on a rate switch. type is jump, left, right, restart, restart_full, death, tps or bugpoint. tps() with no argument is the rate the macro starts at; with a frame it is the rate in effect there, after any switches recorded before it. position is how far playback has advanced.

Read-only on purpose. Handing a script a mutable event list means one bad index can corrupt a recording; editing stays in the macro editor, which keeps the list sorted.

```lua
function on_draw()
  local total = grape.macro.length()
  grape.ui.text(grape.macro.name() .. ": " .. total .. " inputs")
  grape.ui.text("At " .. grape.macro.tps() .. " TPS" ..
    (grape.macro.platformer() and ", platformer" or ""))

  local switches = 0
  for i = 1, total do
    if grape.macro.event(i).type == "tps" then switches = switches + 1 end
  end
  grape.ui.text(switches .. " rate switches")
end
```

Persistent settings
-------------------

```lua
grape.storage.get(key, default) -> value
grape.storage.set(key, value)
```

Boolean, number and string settings are saved per script in scripts/settings. Writes are grouped briefly while a slider is moving and restored on the next load.

Frame-window circle example
---------------------------

```lua
local marker = nil
local color = { 0.2, 1.0, 0.25 }
local tested_delays = 10
local horizon = 90

function on_update(dt)
  marker = nil
  if not grape.in_level() or grape.player_get(1, "dead") then return end

  local action = grape.player_get(1, "holding") and "release" or "click"
  local available = 0
  for delay = 0, tested_delays - 1 do
    if grape.trajectory(1, action, delay, horizon).survived then
      available = available + 1
    end
  end
  marker = {
    x = grape.player_get(1, "x"),
    y = grape.player_get(1, "y") + 35,
    available = available
  }
end

function on_draw()
  color[1] = grape.ui.slider("Circle red", color[1], 0, 1)
  color[2] = grape.ui.slider("Circle green", color[2], 0, 1)
  color[3] = grape.ui.slider("Circle blue", color[3], 0, 1)
end

function on_overlay()
  if not marker then return end
  local strength = marker.available / tested_delays
  grape.level.circle(marker.x, marker.y, 5 + marker.available,
    color[1] * (1 - strength), color[2] * strength, color[3], 0.85)
end
```

Security and limits
-------------------

Scripts run on the game thread with an instruction budget. File, operating-system, package-loading and debug libraries are disabled. A script can still change gameplay state and crash a level through invalid values, so only load scripts you trust. JavaScript is not a second runtime: one Lua API keeps bindings, lifecycle behavior and security rules consistent.
